  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Increasing chances for strong storms with heavy rain today \nand Saturday, with hot temperatures expected to end the weekend\nand start next week. \n\nThe large scale pattern continues to remain largely unchanged\nfrom earlier in the week, with upper level ridging remaining\nquasi-stationary to our southwest and upper trough over the\nnortheast with clusters of storms with embedded vorticity tracks\nfrom the Midwest into the mid-Atlantic. A stronger mid level\nvort max looks to exit the Ohio valley today, and enter the\nregion during peak heating. This mid level vort max looks to\ntrigger storms along a stationary front that has generally\nremained in the vicinity of the Virginia and North Carolina\nborder. This boundary looks to remain in this vicinity through\nthe weekend, with additional storms forming along this boundary\nagain on Saturday. \n\nOverall today, seep layer shear looks to remain around 20 knots.\nThis should allow for multicellular clusters to develop across\nthe region. The environment by this afternoon looks to have\nDCAPE values in the 700-1000 J/Kg range, with SBCAPE values in\nthe 2000-2700 J/Kg range. These values will be enough to support\nstrong to damaging wind gusts with stronger storms that develop\nalong this boundary. Along with the severe weather potential,\nthe environment continues to remain quite moist, with PWATS in\nthe 1.5-1.8 inch range areawide. This can lead to substantial\nrainfall rates in the 3-5 inch per hour range, which will\nquickly lead to flash flooding of small streams, roadways, and\nurban areas if they remain over the same area for too long. \n\nThe threat for an MCS impacting the area has reduced some, but\ncontinues to remain. CAM guidance does not show any MCS moving\ninto the region; however, the upper level and mid level pattern\ncontinues to support it. The main difference compared to earlier\nin the week is the lack of deep layer shear present across the\nregion. As the upper level ridge continues to slide east slowly,\nthe influence of the ridge will inevitably reduce shear and any\nchances for MCS to impact the region. This threat will likely\nshift north of the region where the stronger mid and upper level\njet is expected to lift to. \n\nNot much change in the forecast though looks less active Sunday\nwith ridge building a little more. As this happens, temperatures\nwill continue to rise, with near Heat Advisory criteria levels\npossible across the Piedmont of Virginia and North Carolina on\nSunday and Monday. By Monday evening the ridge gets flattened  \nwith more shortwaves moving across the area once again. The \nflow being more westerly may keep storm coverage for east of \nthe mountains somewhat limited.",
